-- title:
--   Local shape at v of a normalising prime Hecke element
-- statement:
--   Fix $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and a prime $q'$ such that $\mathbb{H}=\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfies `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q'`: $a<0$, $b<0$, and for every height-one prime $w$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the completion $\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_w$ is a division algebra (every nonzero element is a unit) exactly when $q'\in w$. Let $N\neq 0$ and let $R\subseteq\mathbb{H}$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule which is an Eichler order of level $N$, i.e. $R=\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_2$ for maximal orders $\Lambda_1,\Lambda_2$ with the relative index of $R$ in $\Lambda_1$ equal to $N$. Let $r$ be a prime with $r\neq q'$ and $r\nmid N$, and let $v$ be a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $r\in v$. Let $n$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$ lying in `primeHeckeSet R r`, that is: $n$ and $r\,n^{-1}$ lie in the finite adelic box of $R$, while $n^{-1}$ and $r^{-1}n$ do not. Assume further that $n$ normalises the meet order $S=R\cap \mathrm{conjByFiniteIdele}(R,n)$, in the sense that $\mathrm{conjByFiniteIdele}(S,n)=S$. Write $n_v$ for the image of $n$ in $(\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v)^{\times}$ under evaluation at $v$, and for an order $\Lambda$ let $\mathrm{level}(\Lambda,v)$ be the subgroup of $(\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v)^{\times}$ generated by the units $u$ with $u,u^{-1}$ in the local box of $\Lambda$ at $v$ together with the image of $\mathbb{Q}_v^{\times}$. The conclusion is threefold: $n_v^{2}\in\mathrm{level}(R,v)$; $n_v\notin\mathrm{level}(R,v)$; and for every $k\in(\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v)^{\times}$ one has $k\in\mathrm{level}(R,v)$ and $n_v^{-1}kn_v\in\mathrm{level}(R,v)$ if and only if $k\in\mathrm{level}(S,v)$.
--
--   This records the local behaviour at the split prime $v$ of an Atkin–Lehner-type element: $n_v$ normalises the Iwahori-like level of the meet order, inverts the corresponding edge (its square, but not $n_v$ itself, lies in the vertex level), and the intersection of the two vertex levels it connects is exactly the level of the meet order. It is the input used to identify quotients of the vertex and edge sets of the Čerednik–Drinfeld coset graph with quaternionic class sets, and to compute the order of the stabiliser of a dart.
